\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 1 \'97 TITLE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 Hi, I'm Kyaw Min Thu. I'm a controls engineer working on multi-domain industrial systems \'97 where control logic interacts directly with mechanical systems, gas flow, and physical processes. My focus is diagnosing failures that are not obvious, especially when they cross multiple domains.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Pause \'97 look at audience]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 2 \'97 WHAT I DO\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 When systems fail, the first assumption is usually that the PLC or software is the problem. But in my experience, that's often not the case. Most failures originate in the physical system \'97 pressure, flow, or mechanical behavior \'97 and the control system is just reacting.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Slow down]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 So my approach is to identify which domain is actually failing \'97 before making changes.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 3 \'97 BACKGROUND (OFFSHORE)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 I started working on offshore hydraulic systems \'97 specifically top drive systems. These involve hydraulic pressure, mechanical load, electrical control, and safety systems. In that environment, you cannot guess \'97 you must isolate the problem domain first.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Key line]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 "Correct control logic does not guarantee correct system behavior."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 4 \'97 TRANSITION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 That experience shaped how I approach troubleshooting. I don't start by changing logic. I start by understanding system behavior and identifying the responsible domain.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 5 \'97 RCA PHILOSOPHY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 My approach to root cause analysis is structured. I separate symptoms from root causes, and I validate each domain independently.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Point to slide while speaking]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 Control... Electrical... Mechanical / Process.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Key line]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 "A system can behave correctly from the control perspective \'97 and still fail due to process conditions."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 6 \'97 RCA FRAMEWORK\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 I follow a consistent process:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Define the problem\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Isolate domains\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Collect data\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Validate root cause\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Fix and prevent\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Pause]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 The most important step is data collection. Without data, troubleshooting becomes guesswork.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 7 \'97 CASE 1: PROBLEM (Fire System)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 This is a fire training simulator \'97 it uses gas, air, and ignition control.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Continue]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 The issue was intermittent ignition failure. Sometimes it started immediately. Sometimes it required multiple attempts.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Key point]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 From the control system perspective \'97 everything looked correct.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 8 \'97 INVESTIGATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 We verified the electrical signals:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf2 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Burner management trigger \uc0\u10003 \cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0\cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Gas valve operation \uc0\u10003 \cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0\cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Air valve operation \uc0\u10003 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 Everything was working correctly from the control side.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Conclusion]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 So control logic was not the root cause.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 9 \'97 ROOT CAUSE 
\f2 \uc0\u9888 \u65039 
\f0  SLOW DOWN HERE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 The issue was insufficient air pressure during ignition. The air filter was clogged \'97 which restricted airflow.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Continue]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 The compressor rebuilt pressure over time \'97 which made the failure appear random.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Key line \'97 PAUSE after]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 "Logic errors don't self-recover. Process issues do."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 10 \'97 SOLUTION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 We replaced the air filter. But more importantly \'97 we added a pressure transmitter to monitor the air system.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Continue]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 We also added SCADA monitoring and a maintenance schedule.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Key line]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 "Fixing the issue is not enough \'97 you need to make the condition visible."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 11 \'97 LESSON (Case 1)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 The key takeaway is simple: If you don't have instrumentation \'97 you don't have visibility.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 12 \'97 CASE 2: PROBLEM (Intermittent Shutdown)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 In this case, the system shut down intermittently. It happened once a day \'97 or a few times per week.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Continue]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 There was no logging. The issue could not be reproduced.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 13 \'97 STRATEGY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 At that point, troubleshooting was not possible.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Pause]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 So the first step was not debugging \'97 it was creating visibility.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Key line]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 "If you can't see the event \'97 you can't solve it."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 14 \'97 IMPLEMENTATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 I built a 24/7 monitoring system using Python.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Continue]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 It tracked:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Stop conditions\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Permissives\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Interlocks\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Commands\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 And sent real-time alerts through Slack.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 15 \'97 ROOT CAUSE (Case 2)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 We found that a manual button was being intermittently triggered by an associate.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Continue]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 This was not visible in standard PLC trending.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Key line]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 "Standard tools were not enough \'97 so I created a monitoring layer."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 16 \'97 COMPARISON\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 These two cases represent different failure types:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Case 1: process issue \uc0\u8594  solved with instrumentation\cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Case 2: intermittent issue \uc0\u8594  solved with monitoring\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Key line]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 "Different problems require different strategies \'97 but both require real data."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 17 \'97 RELEVANCE TO VELO3D\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb4 Systems like Velo3D have similar challenges:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Gas flow\cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Thermal behavior\cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Motion control\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Continue]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 Failures may not originate in control logic \'97 they can come from physical interactions.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Key line]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 "The key is identifying which domain is responsible \'97 before making changes."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 SLIDE 18 \'97 CLOSING\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 [Slow down]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 What I bring is strong root cause analysis capability, cross-domain understanding, and a structured, data-driven approach.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 [Pause]
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb4 I focus on systems where failures are not obvious \'97 and where real data makes the difference.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
